\begin_layout Abstract
Machine learning methods, in particular reinforcement learning, enables
 autonomous learning of policies for technical systems.
 In order to make the technologies more trustworthy, it is of great interest
 to derive humanly-interpretable rules.
 With the help of genetic programming, simple policies can already be created
 from transition samples.
 In order to further increase the acceptance for solutions, we argue that
 it is advantageous if the solution resembles previously known solutions
 which were already developed for many problems.
 We propose to calculate familiarity as the required edits to an expert
 program, which can replace simplicity as complexity metric in genetic programmi
ng.
 As an additional possibility, the option of defining a fixed core in the
 expert program that must be included in all candidate solutions is presented.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The approach was applied on the Mountain Car and the Industrial Benchmark
 in the form of a symbolic regression on transition samples of several neuropoli
cies.
 The evaluation shows that familiar structures can be recognized in familiar
 trees compared to regular ones, without drawback concerning the offline
 or online performance.
 The fixed core structure additionally enabled the preservation of familiar
 structures in very small programs and also accelerated runs with complex
 programs (with more than 25 start nodes).
 
\end_layout

