\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_survey"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, interpretability is defined as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the ability to explain or to present in understandable terms to humans
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 In contrast to performance, interpretability has many manifestations and
 is not easy to determine.
 A deeper analysis is proposed by Doshi-Velez and Kim in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, who recommend to describe the relevant factors of interpretability including
 the following considerations: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
How is the problem formulation incomplete?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At what level is the evaluation being performed? (application, general user
 study, proxy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are task-related relevant factors? (e.g.
 global vs.
 local, severity of incompleteness, level of user expertise, time constraints)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are method-related relevant factors being explored? (e.g.
 form of cognitive chunks, number of cognitive chunks, compositionality,
 monotonicity, uncertainty)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
RL problems are incomplete as they are not fully testable and can be solved
 in various ways.
 The general level of evaluation is not limited, as applications can also
 be learned through proxy models.
 Considering the task-related relevant factors, we are particularly interested
 in global interpretability, as post-hoc explanations for a subset of decisions
 can not explain complex strategies.
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
xx
\end_layout

\end_inset

 As described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI-unified-approach"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The best explanation of a simple model is the model itself; it perfectly
 represents itself and is easy to understand.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 To find a model that is its own best representation, candidate solutions
 are sought directly in an interpretability-constrained feasible region.
 For RL policies, computer programs or, in a larger context, touring complete
 languages are the interface for explicit communication between machines
 and humans that will be used in this work.
 An evolutionary method of finding such policies is genetic programming,
 which will be further presented in xxx 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "gp_Genetic-Programming"
caps "true"

\end_inset

.
 We argue that simple and interpretable policies can often seem like arbitrary
 combinations of operators, as the solution path often deviates strongly
 from the strategy that an expert would choose.
 Understanding those requires a lot of effort, which is not clearly measurable.
 Doshi-Velez and Kim 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 define a cognitive chunk as the basic unit of explanation.
 Complexity measures for formulas in genetic programming only consider the
 amount of mathematical components of a formula (number of chunks), and
 the complexity of the components (form of cognitive chunks).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Machine lecturing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpretable machine learning methods often rely on supplying general results
 for a standardized person.
 However, it is usually not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

everyone
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 who has to understand the strategy, but only a single expert (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:The-interpretability-gap"

\end_inset

).
 We argue, that the level of user expertise can be considered as starting
 point.
 This is similar to human feedback methods, which try to focus on novelties
 rather than explaining every aspect as the pupil will otherwise loose attention.
 We argue, that familiar chunks represent certain and trusted knowledge
 and even provide better compositionality for further chunk attachments.
\end_layout

