The interpretability gap is visualized, metaphorically, by a deep trench
 that lies between man and machine.
 It represents the learning effort of an individual in order to understand
 a policy.
 For optimal interpretability of policies, they should be as close as possible
 to the expert policy while having the performance of the black box.
 To close this gap between the expert's approach and a black box, we want
 to automatically produce similar programs that improve to be as good as
 the black box agent.
 This is not equally difficult to cross everywhere; the more prior knowledge
 one has, the smaller the gap in things that need to be learned.
 The expert stands at the narrowest point, yet the trench still has to be
 crossed.
