\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Genetic programming}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (GP) is an evolutionary algorithm that can automatically create computer
 programs.
 It has become a well known approach for finding interpretable system controllin
g policies since it was first introduced by Koza 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Koza_GP_patent_1990"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 xxx Individuals in the population are ranked with a fitness function to
 select the best individuals for the next generation.
 [xxx So ist es missverständlich.
 Nicht die Gene werden gerankt.] Computer programs are encoded as a set of
 genes, which can be Random optimization like mutation or crossover allow
 new genes to emerge, which improves the pool of genes over generations.
 Improvements occur stochastically, which makes GP a meta-heuristic optimization
 technique that does not necessarily converge to an optimum.
 The composition of a population is crucial and requires a careful balance
 between reproduction of genes through selection, which is important to
 maintain a functioning gene pool, and random mutation, which provides genetic
 diversity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tree Construction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The representation as computational trees allows for an efficient evolution
 procedure.
 Each tree generated must be evaluable, which is also known as 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{closure}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Type conversion between Boolean and numeric values can be applied automatically
 or with strong typing, which requires explicit type conversion (e.g.
 with comparison operators, if-statements) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 We argue that, even though strong typing is implementation-heavy, the resulting
 programs depict a more practicable composition of mathematical components.
 Evaluation safety poses a particular problem, as type-correct expressions
 can be mathematically invalid.
 For frequently affected operators, dummy exits are defined (e.g.
 the 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{division by zero}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 returns one 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$
\backslash
frac{x}{0}
\backslash
rightarrow 1$
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 To avoid changing the essence of other operators too much, faulty trees
 are discarded and replaced randomly.
 
\end_layout

