\begin_layout Chapter
Familiar Genetic Programming
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Familiar-Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following, the implementation of the approach to enhance genetic
 programming with familiarity is presented.
 This requires a pre-given expert solution, a better performing machine
 learning solution, as well as a fitness function and a metric for the amount
 of changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a reference for familiarity, the domain expert's strategy or program
 must be represented as computational tree, which functions as genetic ancestor
 for all programs (origin).
 If possible, care should be taken to ensure that the structure is hierarchical
 so that the most important structural components are as close as possible
 to the root node.
 [xxx] A symbolic regression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "Symbolic-Regression"
caps "false"

\end_inset

) depicts the fitness function, which is performed on transition samples
 that were collected with the (black box) machine learning solution (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Offline-Data-Selection"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter, we will further present the 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Tree Edit Distance} (TED)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 as complexity measure (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Familiarity-Measurement-TED"

\end_inset

), which calculates the number of differences of computational trees.
