\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Olymp reproduction (Pareto reproduction)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the Greek gods who came to earth to reproduce, the Pareto candidates
 were also given the opportunity to intermingle with the current population.
 By chance, solutions already found and their genes can be lost.
 In order not to lose already found solutions permanently, the genes of
 the best candidates are to provide a very small current of the best candidates
 to the population.
 The Olymp reproduction includes in particular the possibility for the origin
 tree to reproduce itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fixed Tree Core 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fixed tree core is a manually selected tree structure which has to be
 in every individual of the population and is not affected by mutations.
 This was implemented to further bind the process to familiar structures
 and extends the TED, which has shown to loose its protective effect with
 very small trees.
 Fixed nodes can be selected top down and must be fully connected with one
 or more mutatable junctions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "img:tree_modifiable_nodes_modified"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the possibility of multiple junctions, the branching factor is very
 high and can lead to very complex randomly created trees if the tree depth
 is used as limiting factor.
 Hence, the node depth is replaced with a layer-based depth, which depicts
 the distance to the next fixed node as depth-limiting factor.
 The depth in the construction process of random trees was replaced with
 a node-count based version, which included both the full- and grow-method.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/inkscape/fix_nodes_mutation.pdf
	scale 80

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A tree with fixed nodes (blue), which remain untouched by evolution.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "img:tree_modifiable_nodes_modified"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To produce generally balanced trees, the initial size of the to-be replaced
 branches must be considered.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, a random number of nodes to be inserted is chosen.
 Depending on the number of nodes located on a layer, the number of nodes
 to be inserted is randomly divided into intervals.
 These subtrees are divided according to the grow or full method up to the
 interval size they are entitled to.
 A buffer of leaf nodes is built up to avoid having to prune the tree randomly
 at the end.
 To counteract the overloading of a page, the nodes for the next level are
 selected randomly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Tree Unification 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Automatic equation cancellation is the process of replacing calculable branches
 with its result.
 This process can shorten a long process of random mutations, but is only
 applied on the best performing individuals as complex branches offers more
 junctions for the mutation of innovative new gene structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We argue that in familiarity-based GP, which relies on recognizing formula
 structures, unification is more important than simplification, as it strongly
 affects the TED which can not take mathematical similarities into account.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Sums are commutative and 
\begin_inset Formula $(a+b)=(b+a)$
\end_inset

 equivalent, but the edit-distance between the two representations is 2.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A unification procedure requires a holistic simplification and unification
 of a formula with symbolic mathematics into a unique ordered scheme (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Float figure
placement h
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/inkscape/sympy_reduction_example.pdf
	scale 80

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Simplifying a formula achieves a minimal representation by eliminating overcompl
ex parts.
 Unifying further requires restructuring and transforming the formula into
 a unified scheme.
 Both initial formula representations $(a+a)*2$ and $a+3 
\backslash
cdot a$ can be simplified differently, but unified only to the result $a
 
\backslash
cdot 4$.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Implementation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tree unification restructures a tree accordingly to a unique order by extracting
 the expression and simplifying it.
 The basic unification scheme is provided by SymPy 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "10.7717/peerj-cs.103"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, a Python library for symbolic mathematics.
 The simplified expression is compiled to an abstract syntax tree, whose
 components are recursively matched to corresponding GP-node patterns to
 reconstruct a new, unified tree (
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
ref{alg:math-simplification}
\end_layout

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the complexity of mathematics, this process is not robust and can
 lead to arity-restrictions (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:sympy-custom-gp-operators"

\end_inset

) or special cases (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:sympy-sonderfälle"

\end_inset

).
 An initial unification of the origin was not evaluated, as the tree structure
 and familiarity may be altered.
 Further, a uniformly order tree is not necessarily an optimal representation
 for mutations, structurally badly placed root top level nodes can limit
 the evolution process.
 Potential benefits of using unified formulas was therefore not further
 evaluated, even though the implemented simplification successfully reduces
 a large amount of Pareto entries.
