\begin_layout Section
Conclusion 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the mountain car, three expert programs were combined with a black box
 SARSA approach, which was both trained for 75 and 200 training episodes,
 as the general experience at after 75 episodes is still poor.
 The IB was tested with one expert program that was combined with a PSONN
 policy.
 Both the SARSA-75 and the PSONN policy featured hard to fit transition
 samples.
 In particular, the industrial benchmark has a sparse state space; moreover,
 the neuropolicy of particle swarm optimization is very volatile, which
 is why no particularly good solutions were found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Findings on familiar GP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments show significant structural differences between individuals
 in familiar and regular GP runs.
 Structures of the origin program can be recognized in familiar candidates;
 which also forms a more homogeneous Pareto front.
 Nevertheless, in some places structures of the expert were also broken
 up and replaced with new formulas, especially with very simple expert programs
 (3-6 nodes) as the TED loose its effect.
 Fixed nodes could fix this problem and further induced candidates that
 were more robust against misruns.
 A positive side effect of the implementation of fixed nodes is that it
 allows an efficient optimization of extensive computational trees, which
 would normally be too complex to be considered.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The offline performance did not differ significantly, the regression error
 was roughly the same in all runs.
 Within the same absolute complexity range, the online performance differed
 slightly, but not in a way that allow robust conclusions.
 A positive side effect is that the search for appropriately large and complex
 strategies has been made possible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another finding is, that further limitations of the search space (fixed
 tree core, less operators, reduced action space) all performed equally
 in terms of regression error and often better in online performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Limitations and Applicability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A general limitation of offline training is that an agent's actual fitness
 often correlates poorly with its regression error, which Hein has already
 described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Besides, the proposed approach is only applicable when the regression error
 of the expert program implies that structural improvements conceivable.
 Is the correlation between their online- and offline performance does not
 correlate, it should not be applied.
 Reasons for this may be that the black box strategy is too sparse to be
 approximated with symbolic regression, or that the experts' strategy is
 fundamentally different from the black box.
 The same applies for GP candidates, which have to reduce the regression
 error significantly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second limitation is related to the edit distance, which in some cases
 turns into a regular complexity metric, especially for small expert program,
 which will be further discussed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Edit-distance-anomalies"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Edit Distance Anomalies 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Edit-distance-anomalies"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of nodes poses some problems as a paradigm for complexity values,
 even after weighting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important distinction has to be made between the inner and outer nodes
 of a tree.
 The inner nodes, which contain the operators, essentially characterize
 a programming tree by representing the computation that is performed, since
 the exact choice of leaf nodes are the logical prerequisite for its fitness
 and can therefore only be used in the appropriate places.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Operators represent the applied computations of a tree and have the most
 debatable implications for interpretability.
 Not only is the total set of operators used of interest, interpretability
 depends on the structure and type of composition, as can be seen in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:if-operator-composition"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{itemize}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	
\backslash
item A high arity results in more leaf nodes and also more complex models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	
\backslash
item Output-amplification and transformations of operators can propagated
 disproportionately through a tree.
 e.g.
 the power function depends strongly on the exponent ($x+y$, $x
\backslash
cdot y$, $x^y$)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	
\backslash
item Specific requirements regarding the input of an operator.
 e.g.
 the exponent of a power $x^y$ should be a natural number
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

	
\backslash
item Recurring patterns in a tree can be recognised more easily
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{itemize}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

