\begin_layout Paragraph
Leaf nodes 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp-Observations-role"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of leaf nodes follows explicitly from the operator tree and can
 be the branching factor, i.e.
 the number of values to be allocated.
 A distinction should be made between observations and numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Constants and numbers are leaf nodes which are rather irrelevant for understandi
ng a formula.
 Even though they are essential for a program to function and optimizing
 their exact values is subject to an important part of the genetic evolution,
 besides some remarkable constants 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

(for example $
\backslash
pi$)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or the general factor 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

(x$0.1$, x$10$, x$10.000$)
\end_layout

\end_inset

, they have no outstanding characteristic.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observations are the nodes around which operators and constants are to be
 arranged in such a way that they map their values as well as possible to
 an output, which is, after all, the sole task of the GP process.
 Since not all available observations have to be used automatically, they
 mainly also represent the dimensionality of the input vector of the model
 that the solution tree represents.
 Dimensionality plays a crucial role for the mathematical space in which
 solutions are sought (curse of dimensionality).
 The same applies to the developer, for whom these observations represent
 a starting point for interpretation, which is contrary to the genetic approach
 with the tree root as the main anchor point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence, especially the set of unique operators should be considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this thesis, we proposed the concept of familiarity for creating optimized
 interpretable reinforcement learning policies, which depicts similarity
 to already known strategies of a domain expert.
 The technique of creating interpretable policies through symbolic regression
 on transition samples often deviate strongly from the strategy or understanding
 a human expert has developed.
 The Tree Edit distance was proposed as familiarity metric to replace the
 absolute amount of nodes as complexity measure for GP programs.
 In order to raise the commitment to the origin tree, fixed nodes were further
 introduced that allow an expert to further bind the evolutionary process
 to the origin program.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experiments have shown that familiarity can replace the paradigm of
 simplicity in genetic programming without major drawbacks.
 Candidates were allowed to recognize original structures easily and also
 allowed better comparability among Pareto candidates.
 The main difficulty concerned a lack of familiarity with some simple expert
 programs, which could be solved by defining a fixed core.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proposed approach is mainly limited by black box solutions that deviate
 too much from the expert, for example because their policy is very sparse.
 Otherwise, it is conceivable that familiarity not only improves the interpretab
ility of the solutions, but also that solutions are found for problems that
 could not previously be solved with symbolic regression due to their high
 complexity, but that can benefit from the expert's prior knowledge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Future Work 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Future-Work"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, two extension options are discussed in more detail.
 The first one concerns the TED (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "TED-improvements"

\end_inset

), the second one proposes an explorative regression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Explorative-Regression"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Differentiating and Weighting the Familiarity of Edits 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "TED-improvements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using a distance measure such as the edit distance, the question emerges
 whether (and how) changes between nodes can be distinguished as changes
 between terminal nodes and operators can be further distinguished.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED offers many extension possibilities which can further address the
 nature of a change, including a weighting of nodes similar to the one used
 in classical GP which was mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 This requires calculating the weighted mapping.
 A subsequent weighting of the mapping of the regular TED does not make
 sense, since it can lead to ambiguous results that do not correspond to
 the minimum distance.
 The weighted TED opens many questions on how to deal with the effect and
 the complexity of altered nodes in the origin tree.
 Implementations might be based on the following ideas:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Ignoring altered nodes and apply the absolute weighting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Dividing all operators into classes, where the cost for changing within
 a class is weakened.
 A potential implementation are the operator families can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:operator-classification"

\end_inset

.
 This allows adaptions in constant nodes and also a distinction between
 basic operator types.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
With if-statements, calculating the edit distance of the child branches
 separately and adding only the largest factor to the distance.
 This procedure is intended to promote the use of if-statements, which have
 positive influence on the composition of a formula, but increases the TED
 due to the high arity of 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Unifying and ordering trees before calculating the TED, as was proposed
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

