\begin_layout Paragraph
Trimming the considered Edits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As already mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "gp-Observations-role"

\end_inset

, constants have a comparatively insignificant role for the interpretability
 or the essence of a formula anyway.
 Observations play an important role for the problem itself, but also result
 from the pure operational tree.
 Therefore, for normal complexity metrics, the only function of evaluating
 leaf nodes is to penalize the branching factor of a tree without much additiona
l effort.
 For distance-based methods, leaf nodes therefore represent a relatively
 increased factor.
 Especially constants have a strong impact, where an otherwise desired optimizat
ion of a value is evaluated as a full-fledged edit.
 This can not only prevent deliberate simplifications, but can even lead
 to the formula components around the variable being adjusted in order to
 be able to continue to use the constant unchanged.
 A simple solution is to exclude constants when calculating the TED.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observations, on the other hand, have a much more important role, especially
 the amount of observations used.
 As the number of outputs are fixed, the number of necessary observations
 represents the dimensionality of the room of possible solutions, which
 is also known in mathematics as the 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{curse of dimensionality}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 when it is too large.
 Since the quantity of operators used is more important than their number,
 the symmetrical distance between the quantities of observations used can
 also be extended and included in the calculation, for example as a sum
 or as a factor; they should therefore not be ignored like the constants
 and can remain a normal part of the TED.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that this approach is based on the same edit-distance paradigm, but
 contradicts the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

identity of indiscernibles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

-condition of distance measurements.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Explorative Regression
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Explorative-Regression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Explorative regression describes the deviation from the previous policy
 for fitness improvements.
 We argue that, among comparable candidates, less deviation from the known
 is preferred, especially in security-critical domains.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Example: An expert cooks eggs for 10 minutes and a black box for 5 minutes.
 When considering whether we should cook our eggs for 3 minutes and 7, 7
 minutes would be preferable as an interim solution, because the extent
 of the changes to our already known cooking behavior is less.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 For example, a subdivision into four areas is conceivable, as can be seen
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:relative_kernel_path"

\end_inset

, which can be further customized (e.g.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:exploression-regr1"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:exploression-regr2"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

